"3d9c9a20-208b-5bf1-89b3-8f27461cb543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067070 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067070 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067070 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "58197747-933e-54cf-ae88-668f447c54a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067071 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067071 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067071 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7274c8da-3be2-5d2d-aed6-509173699a6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068582 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068582 #edb/ref 1071 "Is $x=3$ a root of the polynomial $2x^3+8x^2-2x-8?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068582 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4968972d-3bc9-5100-aa70-46213e0c3581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068582 #edb/ref 1074 "Substituting $3$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2(3)^3+8(3)^2-2(3)-8 &= \\\n\t\t2(27)+8(9)-6-8 &= \\\n\t\t54+72-14 &= \\\n\t\t112\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $x.$\n\nTherefore, $3$ is *not* a root." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068583 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068583 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $p$ if $3$ is a root of $x^3-3x+p.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1830274-3260-5bf5-aef1-c855a89efbf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068583 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x=3$ is a root, we have $f(3)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=3,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{3}-3(3)+p & =0 \\\\ 27-9+p & =0 \\\\ p & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068749 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068749 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068749 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068749 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068749 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Values that are Roots of a Given Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068749 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068749 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2b680bd3-e1e3-5ff5-ade3-39977cbdaaf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068750 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068750 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068750 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068750 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068750 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068750 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving for an Unknown Parameter Given the Root of a Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068750 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068750 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9328cc9a-2e6c-5ac2-bae4-f02c86fe9d29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068994 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068994 #edb/ref 1045 "Determining the Roots of Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068994 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068994 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068994 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068994 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "484aecae-ce38-5933-8c1b-d7a4869c93b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069305 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069305 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069305 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069305 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $a$ if $-2$ is a root of ${x}^{4}+a{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+60x+100.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069305 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18a12e6a-b89b-57fc-8194-94116cfc4da1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069305 #edb/ref 1074 "Since  $x=-2$ is a root, we have $f(-2)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=-2,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{4}+a(-2)^{3}-11(-2)^{2}+60(-2)+100 & =0 \\\\ 16-8a-44-120+100 & =0 \\\\ -8a-48 & =0 \\\\ -8a & =48 \\\\ a & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069316 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069316 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069316 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069316 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is **NOT** a root of the polynomial ${x}^{4}-13{x}^{2}+36?$\n\nI. $x=4$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=-4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069316 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b7c4071-ca3e-544d-9aa0-e1f8f9fa4fb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069316 #edb/ref 1074 "- Substituting $4$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{4}-13(4)^{2}+36 & = \\\\ 256-13(16)+36 & = \\\\ 256-208+36 & = \\\\ 84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4}-13(2)^{2}+36 & = \\\\ 16-13(4)+36 & = \\\\ 16-52+36 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $2$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $-4$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{4}-13(-4)^{2}+36 & = \\\\ 256-13(16)+36 & = \\\\ 256-208+36 & = \\\\ 84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-4$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $-4$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the values that are *not* roots are $x=4$ and $x=-4,$ so the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069651 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069651 #edb/ref 1061 18472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069651 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7bf976d5-f5da-5111-a8db-afd7667d6464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069651 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069652 #edb/ref 1061 18473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43f1293f-5871-5104-98bf-b58f5a85bbdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069653 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069653 #edb/ref 1061 18475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2f6b6b9-edee-5a14-9900-fb89f4ef499a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069654 #edb/ref 1061 18476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069654 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7174685e-b638-5eb0-a858-a847b1762b27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069654 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069968 #edb/ref 1064 "A **root** of a polynomial is a value that will make the polynomial equal zero when substituted for the polynomial's variable.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $x^2-9.$ This polynomial has two roots, $x=3$ and $x=-3,$ because the polynomial evaluates to $0$ when either of these values is substituted for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}-9 & = \\\\ (3)^{2}-9 & = \\\\ 9-9 & = \\\\ 0 & \\,✓ \\\\ x=-3\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}-9 & = \\\\ (-3)^{2}-9 & = \\\\ 9-9 & = \\\\ 0 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, a number like $x=1$ is *not* a root of the polynomial because the polynomial does not evaluate to $0$ when this value is substituted for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}-9 & = \\\\ (1)^{2}-9 & = \\\\ 1-9 & = \\\\ -8 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069968 #edb/ref 1065 3321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069968 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5b765b45-ae0c-5c2f-958b-e69d41100abc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069968 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069969 #edb/ref 1064 "A **parametric polynomial** is a polynomial in which one of the coefficients is itself represented as another variable.\n\nFor example, the polynomial below is a parametric polynomial:\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x^2 -px\n$$\n\nHere, $p$ is a **parameter**,  which means that it corresponds to a certain constant value. Unlike the variable $x,$ the parameter is treated exactly like any other coefficient. It's only special in that we don't know its numerical value.\n\nIf we're given the root of a polynomial, we can find the numerical value of a parameter. Let's say, for instance, that a root of the above polynomial is $x=1.$ This means that the polynomial is zero for that value:\n\n$$\nf(1)=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=1,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =0 \\\\ 2(1)^{2}-p(1) & =0 \\\\ 2-p & =0 \\\\ p & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069969 #edb/ref 1065 3322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069969 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91fb2858-964a-50e9-a2fc-ae2af84b6b20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069969 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving for an Unknown Parameter Given the Root of a Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513605 #edb/ref 1080 "a=-8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513605 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513605 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c26992c-eeda-521a-91c1-51ff8ccc188a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513639 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513639 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513639 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26aca2b6-27fd-5137-9f5a-b761d0340f16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513711 #edb/ref 1080 "p=-11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513711 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513711 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43ca2c7d-d240-55ed-8330-bebe574fc9f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513733 #edb/ref 1080 "p=-7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513733 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513733 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a5f3c4e-7117-5231-97ac-d46e036354a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513795 #edb/ref 1080 "a=6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513795 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513795 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6113f01d-038a-5ec5-b1bc-33c2dc4ce6db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513844 #edb/ref 1080 "p=-8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513844 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513844 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "75caf8e3-d457-5667-b62f-35a6de77d769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513903 #edb/ref 1080 "a=48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513903 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513903 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f8f7c9e-2f92-5ee6-b16c-88a200fa7761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513962 #edb/ref 1080 "a=8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513962 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513962 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a6c1d10e-3180-570f-b6a8-67238002e359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514038 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514038 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514038 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bfaf3574-1d71-5d97-bc40-fa0a286b8c0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514058 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514058 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514058 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c90944c5-320d-596d-8f3d-d4daa4e170c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514074 #edb/ref 1080 "p=8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514074 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514074 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cf097d6d-7e5d-5696-967e-7db28b75519d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514132 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514132 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514132 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e3d73040-b6a3-590a-8f1a-f21f92d2e834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514182 #edb/ref 1080 "p=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514182 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514182 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f8d013c3-4d16-528c-96a4-cec120511278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514185 #edb/ref 1080 "a=-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514185 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514185 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fae75ffa-784f-514f-a697-d19f80fd75b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514206 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514206 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514206 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fed866e1-3125-5187-99bc-41e67b687920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514298 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514298 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514298 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514298 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514298 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514298 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514298 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514298 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186514132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514298 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "973ef37b-f85d-515c-b5ff-cf8e3417a87e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514341 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514341 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514341 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186513733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514341 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee58cba8-ea3f-5e96-a12e-8748931de619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514357 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514357 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114096" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514357 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514357 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is **NOT** a root of the polynomial ${x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-21x+45?$\n\nI. $x=3$\n\nII. $x=4$\n\nIII. $x=5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514357 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01d0ba8c-f3fb-52e6-bc68-8e1659694309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514357 #edb/ref 1074 "- Substituting $3$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3}-{3}^{2}-21(3)+45 & = \\\\ 27-9-63+45 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $3$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $4$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3}-{4}^{2}-21(4)+45 & = \\\\ 64-16-84+45 & = \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $5$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{3}-{5}^{2}-21(5)+45 & = \\\\ 125-25-105+45 & = \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $5$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $5$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the values that are *not* roots are $x=4$ and $x=5,$ so the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514421 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514421 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514421 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $p$ if $5$ is a root of $f(x)=6{x}^{2}+4px-10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6dc4cf16-d154-5217-ba51-01445eca1e9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514421 #edb/ref 1074 "Since  $x=5$ is a root, we have $f(5)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=5,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(5)^{2}+4p(5)-10 & =0 \\\\ 6(25)+20p-10 & =0 \\\\ 150+20p-10 & =0 \\\\ 140+20p & =0 \\\\ 20p & =-140 \\\\ p & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
